.TH BCC09-CPP 1 2026-11-11 "bcc09 0.13.3" "bcc09 Manual"
.SH NAME
bcc09\-cpp \- the C preprocessor of bcc09 (cpp)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I prefix\c
.B /libexec/bcc09/cpp
.RB [ \-A | \-K ]
.RB [ \-P ]
.RB [ \-D\c
.IR name [= value ]]
.RB [ \-U\c
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-I\c
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpp
is the C preprocessor that
.BR bcc09 (1)
runs first on each C file (and on .S and .x assembly files): it includes
files, defines and expands macros, and keeps or drops lines by
.BR #if ,
.B #ifdef
and the rest, as C89 says, writing the result to
.I output
(or standard output) with
.B #
lines that give each line's file and number, as
.B #line
can too.
It is dev86's cpp; one normally runs it through
.BR "bcc09 \-E" ,
which gives it the include directories and macros for its target.
.PP
A directive that it can't make sense of is an error, naming the file,
the line and the directive.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-A
ANSI C (the driver gives it unless
.BR \-kr ).
.TP
.B \-K
K&R C: no
.B __DATE__
and
.BR __TIME__ .
.TP
.B \-P
Leave out the
.B #
lines that give the line numbers.
.TP
.BI \-D name\c
.RI [= value ]
Define a macro (as 1 if there is no value).
.TP
.BI \-U name
Undefine a macro.
.TP
.BI \-I dir
Look for #include files in
.I dir
too.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
The output file.
.TP
.B \-O
Define
.BR __OPTIMISED__ .
.TP
.B \-c
Define
.BR __CALLER_SAVES__ .
.TP
.B \-f
Define
.BR __FIRST_ARG_IN_X__ .
.TP
.B \-d
Print the tokens, for debugging.
.TP
.B \-T
Pass on only some of the tokens (dev86's; not used).
.TP
.B \-C
Not done (comments are always removed).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 if there were errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bcc09 (1),
.BR bcc09\-unproto (1),
.BR bcc09\-cc1 (1)
